On Fri, 2016-09-30 at 05:01 -0700, Hèctor Perpiñán wrote:
> We
> weren't aware that the prior specifications could not worked if they
> were placed out of the f() functions. Is this documented at any
> place? since this was right long before, is this right?

should be yes. the prior spesification for each 'f' is done inside that
f, like

	f(..., hyper = ...)

while the prior spesification for the likelihood(s) are done in
corresponding 'control.family' list, like for one likehood

control.family = list(..., hyper = ...)

and for two

control.family = list(list(..., hyper = (for the first)),
                      list(..., hyper = (for the second))

> Finally, our examples make evident the problems that could entail the
> use of "non-informative" Gamma priors on the precisions of the BYM
> model. Namely, for scarcer data the posterior mean goes straight to
> the prior mean of the "non-informative" prior distribution which does
> not happen with the uniform distribution on the sd. Taking results of
> this kind into account, have you thought on changing the default
> prior  distributions for those precisions to less dangerous proposals
> (such as the uniform prior on the sds)? In my opinion this would not
> be a foolish idea.
>

You are touching into something interesting, and an exponential on
stdev is a good choice, for the reasons discussed here: https://arxiv.o
rg/abs/1403.4630  (version 3 is the better one),  and for BYM its
here:

https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.01180

this is the default in the model 'bym2'

let me know...

H

